page guide_worktrees {
  title = "Worktree labs"
  chapter_header "Worktree labs" {
    kicker       = "The guide · how-to"
    reading_time = "11 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-29"
    version      = "vmlab 0.35"
  }

  p <<DOC
    Provisioning a lab can take hours: a domain promoted, a database seeded,
    an IDE installed. A linked git worktree normally runs its own
    [instance](guide_architecture) of the lab and builds every machine from
    its template again. A **worktree lab** skips that. You freeze the main
    checkout's lab once at an offline snapshot. Every linked worktree's
    `vmlab up` then builds each machine as a linked clone of the frozen
    disk, starting as if that snapshot had just been restored. The main lab
    keeps running while worktree labs come and go, and no lab in the family
    can reach another. The verbs are in [vmlab worktree](cmd_worktree).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Nothing in `vmlab.wcl` declares a freeze. It is working data in the main
    checkout's `.vmlab/`, so no branch can commit or carry one.
  DOC

  h2 "What this covers"

  list {
    li "freezing the main checkout's lab at an offline snapshot,"
    li "checking that the family runs isolated,"
    li "bringing up a worktree lab that clones its machines from the freeze,"
    li "reaching each lab's fixed ports, which a port slot shifts,"
    li "resetting any lab of the family to the freeze point,"
    li "freezing again after the main lab moves on,"
    li "thawing the freeze back into the main lab."
  }

  p <<DOC
    You need a lab that already runs in the main checkout of a git
    repository, and a linked worktree made with `git worktree add`.
  DOC

  h2 "Freeze the main lab"

  p <<DOC
    Run the freeze in the main checkout, with the lab stopped and an
    **offline** snapshot of the same name on every machine. An online
    snapshot is refused: its memory holds the main lab's DHCP leases and
    agent sessions, and a clone booting its disk alone would see a power cut.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
cd ~/src/app
vmlab down
vmlab snapshot create provisioned
vmlab worktree freeze provisioned
SRC
  }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
froze generation 1 at "provisioned": dc01, web, db
snapshots taken before it are locked until `vmlab worktree thaw`; linked worktrees now clone these machines on `vmlab up`
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Each disk becomes read-only under `.vmlab/freeze/1/`, and the main lab
    carries on in an overlay over it, in exactly the state it had. A failure
    on any disk puts every machine back as it was.
    [vmlab worktree freeze](cmd_worktree) lists what is copied and every
    refusal.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    On a filesystem with reflinks, such as Btrfs or XFS, the freeze copies
    nothing. On any other, it copies every disk in full and keeps the copies
    until the whole freeze has committed, and copies the named volumes for
    good, so the lab needs that much free space while it runs.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The snapshot you froze at is now the **freeze point** of every lab in
    the family. Every snapshot the main lab took before the freeze is
    **locked**: it lives inside a frozen file, so restoring, deleting or
    taking it again is refused until you thaw. See
    [Snapshots under a freeze](guide_snapshots).
  DOC

  h2 "Check worktree isolation"

  p <<DOC
    Cloned guests share hostnames, MAC addresses, machine-ids and Windows
    SIDs, so two labs of a family must never share a wire or a route. While
    the main checkout is frozen, the main lab and every worktree lab built on
    it run with worktree isolation:
  DOC

  list {
    li "NAT refuses every new flow to the host itself: loopback, `0.0.0.0/8`, and every address and broadcast address on a host interface. The lab's own smbd stays reachable, so [shared folders](guide_shares) keep working."
    li "Port forwards bind `127.0.0.1` instead of every interface, and a fixed port is shifted by the lab's port slot, as Ports below describes."
    li "A worktree lab's global segments join `<segment>@<worktree>`, named after the git worktree, so they are shared with no other lab of the family. A global segment with a `connect {}` peer refuses `up` in a worktree lab."
  }

  p <<DOC
    Without the first rule, a worktree guest could reach the main lab's port
    forwards through the host's LAN address and land on its own clone. A
    guest can still reach the internet. [Networking](guide_networking) gives
    the rules in full.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A lab daemon decides isolation when it starts. The freeze ran in the main
    lab's daemon, so the next `vmlab up` there replaces that daemon first and
    says so. Then `vmlab status` shows the decision on the line after the
    lab's name:
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab up && vmlab status" }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
lab "app": the main checkout's freeze or a machine's disk changed — restarting the lab daemon to plan its clones again
...
lab "app"
worktree isolation: frozen main lab, generation 1 at "provisioned"; NAT refuses host addresses, forwards bind 127.0.0.1
SRC
  }

  h2 "Bring up a worktree lab"

  p "In a linked worktree, `vmlab up` reads the main checkout's freeze. Nothing in the worktree needs to change:"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
git worktree add ../feat-x
cd ../feat-x
vmlab up
vmlab status
SRC
  }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
lab "app-feat-x"
worktree isolation: worktree "feat-x", generation 1 at "provisioned"; NAT refuses host addresses, forwards bind 127.0.0.1
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Each machine with no disk yet is cloned from the newest generation when
    the freeze holds it under the same `template` or `image` reference. It
    keeps the frozen machine's MAC addresses, firmware variables and TPM
    state, and boots as the main lab would right after restoring the freeze
    point, so its first-boot provision does not run again. Every other
    machine builds from its template as usual: a machine new in this branch,
    or one whose reference changed.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A machine that already has a disk keeps it, including one built before
    the freeze. To rebuild it from the freeze, run `vmlab vm destroy <name>`
    and `vmlab up` again. A [dev machine](guide_dev) cloned from the freeze
    re-seeds its workspace from this worktree's tree before its watch opens.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    When the freeze's files are missing, `up` refuses and names
    `vmlab worktree freeze` as the fix. It never falls back to the
    templates, which would silently skip the provisioning the freeze
    carries.
  DOC

  h2 "Ports"

  p <<DOC
    Every lab of a frozen family binds its forwards on `127.0.0.1`, so two
    labs that declare one fixed `host_port` would collide. Each lab of the
    family holds a **port slot** instead, and a fixed port is shifted by it.
    Automatic ports, where the declaration leaves the host port out, are not
    shifted.
  DOC

  list {
    li "The main lab is slot 0 and always binds exactly the port it declares. When a process outside the family holds that port, the forward fails, as it does outside a freeze."
    li "A worktree lab takes the lowest free slot from 1 the first time it installs a forward with a fixed port, and keeps it until `vmlab destroy` in that worktree releases it."
    li "A fixed port `P` binds `P + slot`. When another lab of the family has that port recorded, or another process holds it, the lab takes the next port above it that is neither. A shift past 65535 fails that forward alone, naming the declared port."
    li "The port a lab gets is recorded and asked for first on every later install, so it stays the same across `down`, `up` and machine restarts."
    li "Runtime forwards added with `Segment.forward` and a fixed host port follow the same rule."
  }

  p <<DOC
    With the main lab declaring `port { host = 18080 container = 80 }`, the
    first worktree lab's `up` prints the port it bound instead, when it
    installs that forward itself:
  DOC

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
"web": container port declared on host port 18080 is bound on 18081 (worktree port slot, PRD §21.7)
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    A forward is often installed after `up` returns, once its machine takes
    a lease. `up` does not print that one: the lab daemon writes the line to
    its log ([vmlab logs](cmd_logs)), and the `forward.installed` event carries
    `declared_port`. [vmlab status](cmd_status) always shows a shifted
    forward as `18081 (18080)`, and `status --json` carries the declared
    port as `declared_port`. A script
    should not compute the port from the slot. It asks for it instead:
  DOC

  code sh { source = "curl -s \"http://127.0.0.1:$(vmlab port web 80)/\"" }

  p <<DOC
    [vmlab port](cmd_port) prints the host port a running machine's forward
    is bound on, in a worktree lab, the main lab or any other lab.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Slots and every fixed port each lab of the family has bound are recorded
    in the main checkout's `.vmlab/worktree-ports.json`, keyed by the git
    worktree's name. The freeze records the main lab's declared fixed ports
    in slot 0, so a worktree never takes one of them while the main lab is
    down. Every change to the file holds an exclusive lock on it, so two
    labs coming up at once cannot pick one port. A thaw deletes it.
  DOC

  h2 "Reset a lab to the freeze point"

  p "The freeze point resets any lab of the family, the main lab included, to the state it was frozen in:"

  code sh { source = "vmlab snapshot restore provisioned" }

  p <<DOC
    The restore stops a running machine and empties its overlays onto the
    frozen files. With no snapshot taken since the freeze, that is instant.
    Offline snapshots taken since then survive; each is carried into the new
    overlay, which takes time in proportion to what it holds. An online one
    cannot be carried, so the restore refuses, naming it: delete it with
    `vmlab snapshot delete` first. The freeze point's name cannot be taken
    again by `vmlab snapshot create`.
  DOC

  h2 "Freeze again"

  p "When the main lab has moved on and the worktrees should start from its new state, freeze again:"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab down
vmlab snapshot create upgraded
vmlab worktree freeze upgraded
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    This freezes the main lab's current overlays as generation 2, backed by
    generation 1, and locks the old freeze point with the other earlier
    snapshots. Worktree labs already built keep generation 1; only machines
    built from now on start from generation 2. A worktree lab's daemon
    that planned its clones before the refreeze is replaced by its next
    `up`, as after an edit to `vmlab.wcl`. To see which worktree uses which
    generation:
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree status" }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
main checkout: /home/wil/src/app
GENERATION  SNAPSHOT     MACHINES       WORKTREES
1           provisioned  dc01, web, db  /home/wil/src/feat-x
2 (newest)  upgraded     dc01, web, db  -
SRC
  }

  h2 "Thaw"

  p "Thawing folds the freeze back into the main lab's disks and unlocks its earlier snapshots. Destroy every worktree lab first, then thaw in the main checkout with the lab stopped:"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
cd ../feat-x && vmlab destroy
cd ../app && vmlab down && vmlab worktree thaw
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    The main lab keeps its current state, and offline snapshots taken after
    a freeze survive. The thaw refuses while any worktree disk backs onto the
    freeze, naming each worktree. It also refuses on online snapshots taken
    since a freeze, whose machine state no commit can carry; delete them, or
    pass `--drop-online-snapshots` to give them up. Isolation ends at the
    main lab's next `up`, which replaces its daemon as after the freeze.
  DOC

  h2 "Guards on a freeze"

  list {
    li "`vmlab destroy` in the main checkout refuses while any worktree lab uses the freeze, naming each one. With none using it, `destroy` deletes the freeze with the rest of `.vmlab/`."
    li "`vmlab lab move` refuses to move a frozen main lab, because every overlay of the family names the frozen files by absolute path. When worktrees depend on the freeze, the refusal names them; otherwise it asks you to thaw first."
    li "The template store counts a template as in use while any frozen file backs onto it, so `vmlab template rm` needs `--force`."
    li "Nothing in vmlab opens a frozen file read-write."
  }

  callout "A freeze is not portable" {
    kind = :error
    body = "Worktree overlays name the frozen files by absolute path. Moving or renaming the main checkout's directory, or its `VMLAB_WORK_DIR`, breaks every worktree lab built on it. Destroy the worktree labs and thaw first."
  }

  p <<DOC
    A freeze needs the main lab stopped; there is no live freeze. A lab run
    with `--instance` or `VMLAB_INSTANCE` in an ordinary directory is not a
    worktree lab and keeps the ordinary instance rules. To share a
    provisioned machine across hosts, build a [template](guide_templates)
    from it instead.
  DOC

  h2 "See also"

  list {
    li "[vmlab worktree](cmd_worktree): the freeze, thaw and status verbs, what they copy, their output and refusals."
    li "[vmlab port](cmd_port): the host port a forward is bound on, shifted or not."
    li "[vmlab snapshot](cmd_snapshot): how the freeze point and locked snapshots restore."
    li "[Networking](guide_networking): worktree isolation beside the NAT, forwards and global segments it changes."
    li "[How vmlab runs a lab](guide_architecture): lab instances, which every worktree lab is first."
  }
}
